WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.029
Prompt chaining: split the work into steps you can check.

2
00:00:03.309 --> 00:00:09.325
Even with strong reasoning models, some tasks are better split into several calls, each with one job.

3
00:00:09.605 --> 00:00:19.376
Anthropic’s best-practices page says explicit chaining remains useful when you need to inspect intermediate outputs or enforce a particular pipeline.

4
00:00:19.656 --> 00:00:34.632
Anthropic’s “Building effective agents” essay describes prompt chaining as a workflow where each call processes the previous call’s output, with programmatic checks (it calls them gates) between steps, trading some latency for accuracy.

5
00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:36.789
Back to the status-report example.

6
00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:46.157
One giant prompt that reads a week of updates and writes the executive summary gives you one output to judge, and when a date is wrong you can’t tell why.

7
00:00:46.437 --> 00:00:48.677
A chain gives you three checkable steps.

8
00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:52.861
Step one extracts every milestone, owner, and date as JSON.

9
00:00:53.141 --> 00:00:59.200
A code gate checks that the JSON parses and that each date string actually appears in the source updates.

10
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.403
Step two writes the summary from the checked list only.

11
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:09.680
Each step has its own test cases, and you can run the cheap extraction on a smaller model if your tests show it holds up.

12
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:21.139
The most common chain, according to Anthropic’s docs, is self-correction: draft, then have the model review the draft against explicit criteria, then revise based on the review.

13
00:01:21.419 --> 00:01:25.643
Each step is a separate call, so you can log it, score it, or stop early.
